;;; A wildcard `_` as a pattern's relation head stopped the run with a Python
;;; spelling — M1e2 **W20**, register row `W20/wild-in-head`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by S1e2.2.20 (2026-11-05) from the
;;; user's ruling of 2026-11-04 (Q-M1e2.13 (a)).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(holds a)`, and one rule that concludes `(hit w)` from any
;;; fact whose one argument is `a`, whatever its relation: the pattern's
;;; head is the wildcard `_`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A relation is named by a name or by a parameter its activator binds
;;; (Q29), and `_` stands for an argument: the user ruled on 2026-11-05 that
;;; a wildcard written as a relation is refused at load, as a range is
;;; (Q-M1e2.14 (c)), with a line in Ein's spelling naming the rule and the
;;; slot. Every command that loads the program exits 1 (`ein test`: 2). Until
;;; that ruling the right answer was `wild-in-head-twin.ein`'s, `(_ a)`
;;; matching every fact of one argument `a` (Q-M1e2.13 (a)); a fresh head
;;; variable is no twin, since M1 refuses `(?r a)` with no parameter `?r` as
;;; it refused `_`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.20 every command that compiled the rule exited 1: `ein
;;; saturate` with `ein.inference.compile.CompileError: unbound relation head
;;; ?Wildcard() in a premise of `SForm(head=Wildcard(), args=(Atom(name='a'),))`
;;; — M1 matches relations per activator (Q29), …`, and `ein solve` and `ein
;;; oracle` with the message alone, the Python IR's spelling of the pattern
;;; (`ein solve FILE` at `c73a0796`, release and debug). Since then the loader
;;; refuses it: *kb load error: rule 'w': its :match writes (_ a) with the
;;; wildcard _ as its relation — a relation is named by a name or by a
;;; parameter its activator binds, and _ stands for an argument*.

(relation holds Node) (relation hit Node)
(holds a)
(rule w () :match (_ a) :assert (hit w))
